// Subsurface rod pumps and the dynamometer card they draw (5.90, D1 increment 4).
//
// A rod-pump row is a point element with two ports: the intake below the
// plunger and the tubing shoe just above it. Its rate is not an answer to a
// pressure difference -- it is the barrel's swept volume, A_p (S - delta_f) N,
// priced on the string's own elasticity -- and what it reports back is the
// differential its two node pressures happen to differ by. So the whole card
// falls out of the network solve rather than out of an assumed gradient: the
// annulus fluid level, the casing pressure, the tubing's weight and friction and
// the wellhead backpressure all arrive already added up into A_p * dp.
//
// Each well arm is the same three rows around the pump:
//   ANNn  PUMPn  from Pn (intake) up the h-metre liquid column to Hn, the
//         annulus fluid level, held at the casing pressure. The column carries
//         the difference between what the formation feeds and what the pump
//         takes, so its friction is part of the intake pressure, exactly as it
//         is in the well.
//   PUMPn Pn -> Dn, length 0. The machine.
//   TUBn  Dn -> Wn, the produced column of the setting depth L up to the
//         wellhead, carrying the pump's own rate.
// which is the pressure the plunger works against in closed form:
//   dp = (p_W - p_H) + rho g (L - h) + dpFric(tubing) - dpFric(annulus)
// and A_p times that is the fluid load. The columns are laminar (rho 850, mu
// 0.1) so each friction drop is the explicit 128 mu L Q / (pi D^4) and the
// checker never iterates to re-derive a card.
//
// Arms, and what each one is for:
//   RP_FLOOD  annulus full to surface (h = L): the column's weight is all taken
//             off underneath, so the fluid load is only the 10 bar across the
//             two heads plus friction -- the thin flooded card, and the largest
//             upthrust of the set.
//   RP_DEPR   the archetype: level 600 m below surface, 1.5 m stroke, 8 spm,
//             1.75 in barrel on a 3/4 in string at 1500 m. The load stretches
//             the string by a quarter of the stroke, so the volumetric
//             efficiency is ~0.83 -- the number a shot reading is for.
//   RP_GAUGE  RP_DEPR's well with a 7/8 in string: same load, less stretch,
//             longer effective stroke, heavier rod weight. The gauge lever.
//   RP_DEPTH  RP_DEPR at 2500 m with the level 1500 m deep: Fo_max and Wr both
//             scale with the depth, the stretch constant per unit load does not.
//   RP_STALL  RP_DEPTH's well with a 5/8 in string and a 1.0 m stroke: the load
//             stretches the string further than the stroke, so the plunger never
//             leaves bottom dead centre -- STALLED, S_eff 0, no volume.
//   RP_IDLE   the same well as RP_DEPR with the annulus charged to 9 MPa: the
//             underside outranks the tubing side, dp < 0, both valves stand open
//             and there is no fluid load at all -- the flat NO-LOAD line at the
//             buoyed rod weight, at zero work.
//   RP_BUOY   RP_DEPR with a 700 kg/m3 (fibreglass) string lighter than the
//             fluid it hangs in: the buoyed weight is negative, so the card
//             straddles the zero-load axis. The arithmetic does not care which
//             is heavier, and the element says so instead of clamping.
//   RP_FAST   RP_DEPR's pump at 12 spm: the load, the stretch and the efficiency
//             are statics and do not move with speed -- only the column friction
//             the extra rate costs does, and that is the whole second-order
//             footprint of N on the card.
//   RP_RIG    the inverse problem: a metered feed of 2.8e-4 m3/s into the intake
//             and a riser to the separator. The feed closes the pump's own
//             displacement equation, so the network must find the differential
//             whose stretch leaves exactly that stroke -- delta_f = S - Q/(A_p N).
//   RP_ANNFRI the same well as RP_DEPR with the formation feed shut off, so the
//             whole produced rate comes down the annulus through a narrow bore
//             and its 1.1 MPa of friction is part of the intake pressure: the
//             card absorbs a sub-plunger friction, not only a hydrostatic level.
//
// The delta_f of a 1.75 in barrel at 1500 m on a 3/4 in string is 1 m of stretch
// per 24.49 MPa of differential, so no arm here sits within 20 percent of a
// state boundary: the FULL arms carry between 0.05 and 0.32 m of stretch, the
// stalled arm is 32 percent past its stroke, and the idle arm is 1.9 MPa the
// wrong way round.

fluid
{
    rho 850; mu 0.1;
}

nodes
{
    // ---- the wells: intake and discharge solved, level and head fixed ------
    P1 {} D1 {} H1 { pressure 1.0e6; }  W1 { pressure 2.0e6; }
    P2 {} D2 {} H2 { pressure 1.0e6; }  W2 { pressure 2.0e6; }
    P3 {} D3 {} H3 { pressure 1.0e6; }  W3 { pressure 2.0e6; }
    P4 {} D4 {} H4 { pressure 1.0e6; }  W4 { pressure 2.0e6; }
    P5 {} D5 {} H5 { pressure 1.0e6; }  W5 { pressure 2.0e6; }
    P6 {} D6 {} H6 { pressure 9.0e6; }  W6 { pressure 2.0e6; }
    P7 {} D7 {} H7 { pressure 1.0e6; }  W7 { pressure 2.0e6; }
    P8 {} D8 {} H8 { pressure 1.0e6; }  W8 { pressure 2.0e6; }
    P10 {} D10 {} H10 { pressure 1.0e6; } W10 { pressure 2.0e6; }

    // ---- the flow test: a metered feed, a riser, and nothing else ----------
    P9 {}  D9 {}  W9 { pressure 2.0e6; }
}

sources
{
    // The formation's own contribution to each intake, and the test stand's
    // metered feed. These are what the pump's rate is balanced against; they
    // also set the residual scale the solve is judged on.
    f1  { node P1;  rate 3.0e-4; }
    f2  { node P2;  rate 3.0e-4; }
    f3  { node P3;  rate 3.0e-4; }
    f4  { node P4;  rate 3.0e-4; }
    f5  { node P5;  rate 3.0e-4; }
    f6  { node P6;  rate 1.0e-4; }
    f7  { node P7;  rate 3.0e-4; }
    f8  { node P8;  rate 4.5e-4; }
    f9  { node P9;  rate 2.8e-4; }
    f10 { node P10; rate 0.0; }
}

pipes
{
    // ---- 1. flooded annulus ------------------------------------------------
    ANN1  { from P1; to H1; length 1500; diameter 0.151; elevation 1500; }
    RP_FLOOD
    {
        from P1; to D1; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.5;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 8; rodDiameter 0.019050;
        rodLength 1500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 7850;
    }
    TUB1 { from D1; to W1; length 1500; diameter 0.062; elevation 1500; }

    // ---- 2. the archetype: level 600 m deep --------------------------------
    ANN2  { from P2; to H2; length 900; diameter 0.151; elevation 900; }
    RP_DEPR
    {
        from P2; to D2; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.5;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 8; rodDiameter 0.019050;
        rodLength 1500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 7850;
    }
    TUB2 { from D2; to W2; length 1500; diameter 0.062; elevation 1500; }

    // ---- 3. the same well, a stiffer string --------------------------------
    ANN3  { from P3; to H3; length 900; diameter 0.151; elevation 900; }
    RP_GAUGE
    {
        from P3; to D3; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.5;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 8; rodDiameter 0.022225;
        rodLength 1500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 7850;
    }
    TUB3 { from D3; to W3; length 1500; diameter 0.062; elevation 1500; }

    // ---- 4. 2500 m, level 1500 m deep --------------------------------------
    ANN4  { from P4; to H4; length 1000; diameter 0.151; elevation 1000; }
    RP_DEPTH
    {
        from P4; to D4; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.5;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 8; rodDiameter 0.019050;
        rodLength 2500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 7850;
    }
    TUB4 { from D4; to W4; length 2500; diameter 0.062; elevation 2500; }

    // ---- 5. the same well, a lighter string and a shorter stroke -----------
    ANN5  { from P5; to H5; length 1000; diameter 0.151; elevation 1000; }
    RP_STALL
    {
        from P5; to D5; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.0;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 8; rodDiameter 0.015875;
        rodLength 2500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 7850;
    }
    TUB5 { from D5; to W5; length 2500; diameter 0.062; elevation 2500; }

    // ---- 6. the annulus outranks the tubing: no load -----------------------
    ANN6  { from P6; to H6; length 900; diameter 0.151; elevation 900; }
    RP_IDLE
    {
        from P6; to D6; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.5;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 8; rodDiameter 0.019050;
        rodLength 1500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 7850;
    }
    TUB6 { from D6; to W6; length 1500; diameter 0.062; elevation 1500; }

    // ---- 7. a string lighter than the fluid --------------------------------
    ANN7  { from P7; to H7; length 900; diameter 0.151; elevation 900; }
    RP_BUOY
    {
        from P7; to D7; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.5;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 8; rodDiameter 0.019050;
        rodLength 1500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 700;
    }
    TUB7 { from D7; to W7; length 1500; diameter 0.062; elevation 1500; }

    // ---- 8. the same pump, run faster --------------------------------------
    ANN8  { from P8; to H8; length 900; diameter 0.151; elevation 900; }
    RP_FAST
    {
        from P8; to D8; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.5;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 12; rodDiameter 0.019050;
        rodLength 1500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 7850;
    }
    TUB8 { from D8; to W8; length 1500; diameter 0.062; elevation 1500; }

    // ---- 9. the inverse: an imposed rate finds its own differential --------
    RP_RIG
    {
        from P9; to D9; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.5;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 8; rodDiameter 0.019050;
        rodLength 1500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 7850;
    }
    TUB9 { from D9; to W9; length 1500; diameter 0.062; elevation 1500; }

    // ---- 10. all of it down the annulus ------------------------------------
    ANN10 { from P10; to H10; length 900; diameter 0.030; elevation 900; }
    RP_ANNFRI
    {
        from P10; to D10; length 0; diameter 0;
        rodPlungerDiameter 0.044450; rodStrokeLength 1.5;
        rodStrokesPerMinute 8; rodDiameter 0.019050;
        rodLength 1500; rodYoungModulus 2.0e11; rodDensity 7850;
    }
    TUB10 { from D10; to W10; length 1500; diameter 0.062; elevation 1500; }
}

solve { maxIter 400; tol 1e-11; }
